---
<Base title={`${p.name} — MCP server`} description={p.summary}>
  <p><a href="/#building">← Back</a></p>
  <span class="pill">{p.status}</span>
  <h1 style="margin-top:12px">{p.name}</h1>
  <p class="muted">{p.summary}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <p>It audits MCP servers as software artifacts: registry listings, packages, configs, and tool definitions. It exposes six tools:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>scan_tool_definitions</code>: scans a server’s pasted <code>tools/list</code> JSON for hidden characters, instruction-like phrasing, references to other tools, exfiltration-like wording, encoded blobs, padding, parameters that invite dumping context, and duplicate or look-alike tool names.</li>
      <li><code>audit_registry_entry</code>: checks an MCP Registry listing for consistency, status and ownership markers.</li>
      <li><code>check_typosquat</code>: compares a server or package name against known names for look-alikes.</li>
      <li><code>audit_package</code>: audits a specific PyPI or npm package and version, including known vulnerabilities and package-level red flags.</li>
      <li><code>scan_mcp_config</code>: reviews a pasted client config for risky settings. It takes pasted text only, never a file path, and no config value appears in the report.</li>
      <li><code>audit_server</code>: the full check for one server, combining the others.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <pre><code>uvx mcp-supply-audit</code></pre>
    <p>Or pin the version:</p>
    <pre><code>uvx mcp-supply-audit@0.1.1</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">Or add to your MCP client config — see the repo README.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Never executes a target.</strong> All checks are static. It reads listings, packages, configs, and tool definitions but never runs the server it is auditing.</li>
      <li><strong>Treats input as hostile.</strong> Hidden characters in what it reads are shown as visible escapes, not passed through.</li>
      <li><strong>Deterministic rules.</strong> Detection is rule-based. No LLM is involved in detection.</li>
      <li><strong>Four allowed hosts only.</strong> Network access is limited to four allowed hosts.</li>
      <li><strong>Indicators, not verdicts.</strong> Results are indicators to review, not a safe/unsafe verdict, and any skipped checks are always listed.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Demo</h2>
    <p class="muted">Run 2026-11-11.</p>
    <pre><code>{demoOutput}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Known limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Static analysis only.</li>
      <li>No check of PyPI install scripts.</li>
      <li>The reference list is dated.</li>
      <li>Large PyPI projects need a version specified.</li>
      <li>The OWASP MCP Top 10 mapping is against a beta.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Evaluation</h2>
    <p>Measured 2026-11-09 on the tool-definition rules (TD001–TD008), against 19 positive fixtures and a benign corpus of 9 servers with 77 tools.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Detection:</strong> at least one expected rule fired on 12/19 positives at baseline and 19/19 after tuning. Exact matches went from 6/19 to 15/15 on the in-scope positives (15/19 over every counted pattern), with no extra rules fired.</li>
      <li><strong>Benign:</strong> 0 findings on the 9 servers and 77 tools.</li>
      <li><strong>Caveat:</strong> the post-tuning numbers are in-sample, measured on the same fixtures used for tuning. The baseline is the out-of-sample reference, and a held-out evaluation is still pending.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={`${p.links.repo}#evaluation`}>Full evaluation, known evasions and limits in the README</a></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Links</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={p.links.repo}>Source on GitHub</a></li>
      <li><a href={p.links.pypi}>PyPI package</a></li>
      <li><a href={p.links.registry}>Official MCP Registry entry (JSON)</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

